('math-sum-product-transformations-01','mathematics','trigonometry','Trigonometric functions','Sum-to-product transformations','sin C+sin D equals:','["2sin((C+D)/2)cos((C−D)/2)","2cos((C+D)/2)sin((C−D)/2)","sin(C+D)","2sinC sinD"]',0,'The sum-to-product identity gives 2sin((C+D)/2)cos((C−D)/2).','standard',array['jee-main','tg-eapcet','ap-eapcet'],'published','NCERT Mathematics XI; official JEE Main and EAPCET syllabi',now(),'codex-phase3-review','1.0'),
('math-trig-principal-solutions-01','mathematics','trigonometry','Trigonometric equations','Principal solutions','In [0,2π), the solutions of sin x=0 are:','["0 and π","π/2 and 3π/2","π only","0 and 2π"]',0,'Sine is zero at integer multiples of π; in the half-open interval these are 0 and π.','foundation',array['jee-main','tg-eapcet','ap-eapcet'],'published','NCERT Mathematics XI; official JEE Main and EAPCET syllabi',now(),'codex-phase3-review','1.0'),
('math-trig-general-solutions-01','mathematics','trigonometry','Trigonometric equations','General solutions','The general solution of tan x=0 is:','["x=nπ, n∈Z","x=2nπ+π/2","x=nπ+π/4","x=(2n+1)π/2"]',0,'Tangent vanishes at every integer multiple of π.','foundation',array['jee-main','tg-eapcet','ap-eapcet'],'published','NCERT Mathematics XI; official JEE Main and EAPCET syllabi',now(),'codex-phase3-review','1.0'),
('math-trig-equation-transformations-01','mathematics','trigonometry','Trigonometric equations','Equation transformations','For real x, the equation sin²x+cos²x=1 is:','["an identity true for all x","true only at x=0","true only at x=π/2","never true"]',0,'The Pythagorean identity holds for every real angle.','foundation',array['jee-main','tg-eapcet','ap-eapcet'],'published','NCERT Mathematics XI; official JEE Main and EAPCET syllabi',now(),'codex-phase3-review','1.0'),
('math-inverse-trig-branches-01','mathematics','trigonometry','Inverse trigonometric functions','Principal value branches','The principal-value range of sin⁻¹x is:','["[−π/2,π/2]","[0,π]","(−π,π]","[0,2π)"]',0,'Restricting sine to [−π/2,π/2] makes it one-to-one and defines its inverse.','foundation',array['jee-main','tg-eapcet','ap-eapcet'],'published','NCERT Mathematics XII; official JEE Main and EAPCET syllabi',now(),'codex-phase3-review','1.0'),
('math-inverse-trig-graphs-01','mathematics','trigonometry','Inverse trigonometric functions','Graphs','The graph of y=sin⁻¹x is the reflection of which restricted graph across y=x?','["y=sin x on [−π/2,π/2]","y=cos x on [0,π]","y=tan x on (−π/2,π/2)","y=cot x on (0,π)"]',0,'A function and its inverse have reflected graphs, using the one-to-one sine branch.','foundation',array['jee-main','tg-eapcet','ap-eapcet'],'published','NCERT Mathematics XII; official JEE Main and EAPCET syllabi',now(),'codex-phase3-review','1.0'),
('math-inverse-trig-identities-01','mathematics','trigonometry','Inverse trigonometric functions','Identities and transformations','For x>0, tan⁻¹x+tan⁻¹(1/x) equals:','["π/2","π","0","π/4"]',0,'For positive x the two principal angles are complementary, so their sum is π/2.','standard',array['jee-main','tg-eapcet','ap-eapcet'],'published','NCERT Mathematics XII; official JEE Main and EAPCET syllabi',now(),'codex-phase3-review','1.0'),
